---
// Scaled (1 px = 1 mm) outlines: published device outer sizes vs. interior sizes stated in three Amazon sleeve listings.
const sleeves = [
  { label: 'kwmobile "6-inch" pouch', sub: 'listed inside: 170 × 120 mm', w: 120, h: 170 },
  { label: 'MoKo nylon "6-7 inch"', sub: 'listed inside: 190 × 140 mm', w: 140, h: 190 },
  { label: 'MoKo "6-7 inch" padded', sub: 'listed inside: 200 × 150 mm', w: 150, h: 200 },
];
const devs = [
  { label: 'Paperwhite 2024', w: 127.6, h: 176.7, stroke: '#9a3b1f', dash: '' },
  { label: 'Paperwhite 2026', w: 126.8, h: 174.7, stroke: '#1a5276', dash: '3 2' },
  { label: 'Kindle 2022/2024', w: 108.6, h: 157.8, stroke: '#2f5d57', dash: '5 3' },
];
const cx = [125, 380, 635];
---
<figure class="fig">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 760 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="sl-t sl-d" width="760" height="300">
    <title id="sl-t">Kindle outer sizes against three sleeve interiors, to scale</title>
    <desc id="sl-d">Three dashed-grey rectangles show the interior size stated in a 6-inch neoprene pouch, a nylon 6-7 inch sleeve and a padded 6-7 inch sleeve. Solid and dashed outlines show the 2024 Paperwhite, 2026 Paperwhite and the 2022 or 2024 basic Kindle at the same scale. Both Paperwhites are larger than the 6-inch pouch interior and fit inside the other two.</desc>
    <g font-family="sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#1d1b18" text-anchor="middle">
      {sleeves.map((s, i) => {
        const x = cx[i] - s.w / 2, y = 14;
        return (
          <g>
            <rect x={x} y={y} width={s.w} height={s.h} rx="4" fill="#f3efe4" stroke="#6b665c" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4" />
            {devs.map((d) => (
              <rect x={cx[i] - d.w / 2} y={y + (s.h - d.h) / 2} width={d.w} height={d.h} rx="6" fill="none" stroke={d.stroke} stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray={d.dash || undefined} />
            ))}
            <text x={cx[i]} y={y + s.h + 22} font-weight="700">{s.label}</text>
            <text x={cx[i]} y={y + s.h + 40} font-size="11.5" fill="#4a453d">{s.sub}</text>
          </g>
        );
      })}
    </g>
    <g font-family="sans-serif" font-size="11.5" fill="#1d1b18">
      <line x1="10" y1="278" x2="36" y2="278" stroke="#9a3b1f" stroke-width="2" /><text x="42" y="282">PW 2024 (127.6 × 176.7)</text>
      <line x1="230" y1="278" x2="256" y2="278" stroke="#1a5276" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="3 2" /><text x="262" y="282">PW 2026 (126.8 × 174.7, secondary)</text>
      <line x1="520" y1="278" x2="546" y2="278" stroke="#2f5d57" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5 3" /><text x="552" y="282">Kindle 22/24 (108.6 × 157.8)</text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption>Our own drawing at 1 px = 1 mm. 2024 Paperwhite and basic Kindle sizes are Amazon's published outer dimensions; 2026 Paperwhite footprint is from secondary sources citing Amazon product data. Sleeve sizes are the "internal" figures the sellers state, which one listing says were measured by hand. Padding, lining and zipper edges reduce the usable space in real life, so this shows the best case, not a fit guarantee.</figcaption>
</figure>
